==============================================================================
cmark-gfm (GitHub Flavored Markdown)
==============================================================================

Kelpto's tests include examples extracted from cmark-gfm
(https://github.com/github/cmark-gfm), GitHub's reference implementation
of GitHub Flavored Markdown, at tag 0.29.0.gfm.13 (commit
587a12bb54d95ac37241377e6ddc93ea0e45439b): test/spec.txt, the GFM spec
(SHA-256 7d8e5814befec287ac116786d81ff14e0adc9b13295b4494649e995408fd871c),
and test/extensions.txt, cmark-gfm's tests of the extensions, by Yuki
Izumi (SHA-256
a2a45e98be9fca95f564f927265a0f63beea6cae5369d1cf4bde44caa51b2a3a),
without its footnote examples: each example's Markdown and expected HTML,
its section, and the extension it needs. They are in tests/gfm/spec.json
and tests/gfm/examples.temper.md, a test-only library kept apart from
Kelpto's own code, which nothing imports and which isn't published.
scripts/gfm_spec.py extracts them the way cmark-gfm's test tools
(test/spec_tests.py) read them.

spec.txt and extensions.txt are released under the Creative Commons
CC-BY-SA 4.0 license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/>
(spec.txt is based on the CommonMark spec, Copyright (C) 2014-16 John
MacFarlane), so the extracted examples, adapted from them, are also
under CC-BY-SA 4.0, not under Kelpto's Apache-2.0 OR MIT license.

kelpto-md's GFM extensions follow cmark-gfm's behavior, as its source
describes it; no code is copied. cmark-gfm's code and test software are
